<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">August Hermann Francke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1663">1663</a>–<a href="/years/1727">1727</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">August Hermann Francke (1663–1727) was a German Lutheran clergyman, theologian and educator, a leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a>. As professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a> and pastor at Glaucha, he founded in 1698 the Francke Foundations, a complex of orphanage, schools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> house and pharmacy. His institutions became a model for education and a base for Protestant missions, including to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/august-hermann-francke.jpg" alt="August Hermann Francke" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:August-Hermann-Francke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>August Hermann Francke was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> on 22 March 1663. He was educated at the Illustrious Gymnasium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> and studied at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, where he came under the influence of the Pietist Christian Kortholt, and finally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. He made a special study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Greek and for a time took lessons in Hebrew from Ezra Edzardi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. He graduated at Leipzig, where he became a Privatdozent in 1685, and a year later, with the approval of Philipp Jakob Spener, founded the Collegium Philobiblicum, where graduates met regularly for the systematic study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Francke returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1689 to give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> lectures and became popular, but the university authorities opposed his teaching and before the end of the year forbade him to lecture on the grounds of alleged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a>. In 1690 he became a deacon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, where his preaching drew crowds, Roman Catholics among them, and angered his opponents; on 27 September 1691 the civil authorities ordered him to leave within forty-eight hours. Through Spener's influence he accepted the chair of Greek and oriental languages at the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a> in December 1691 and was also made pastor of Halle-Glaucha. He later became professor of theology and held both offices for the remaining thirty-six years of his life.</p><p>Concerned for the underprivileged children growing up around him in ignorance and crime, Francke resolved in 1695 to found a &quot;ragged school&quot; supported by public charity. Within a year it needed a house, and another was added in 1697. By 1698 he had 100 orphans to clothe and feed and 500 day scholars, and the schools were later known as the Franckesche Stiftungen. Teaching was strictly religious, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> included and the Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> classics neglected, though he also taught natural science, physical exercises and manual trades. The institution ran an apothecary's shop and a press <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> cheap copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> for mass distribution. In 1698 he founded the Gynaeceum, the first Protestant higher girls school, which lasted until 1730.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Francke married Anna Magdalena von Wurm in 1694, and they had three children. He died in Halle on 8 June 1727, when more than 2,300 pupils attended his schools. They provided a prototype that greatly influenced later German education, particularly women's education. Halle became a centre from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a> spread widely over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and under Francke's influence Christian missionary efforts grew, with Halle the centre of the Danish-Halle Mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Among his writings were Manuductio ad Lectionem Scripturae Sacrae (1693) and an account of his orphanage, Segensvolle Fußstapfen (1709).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “August Hermann Francke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nicolaus Zinzendorf, a Saxon noble who had studied under the Pietist leader <b>August Hermann Francke</b>, invited members of the Moravian Church to his estates in 1722, where they founded a community at Herrnhut; it became a refuge for other Protestants as well, and Moravian societies were later established…</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The University of Halle, founded in 1694 in the duchy, became a centre of Pietism and the early Enlightenment under <b>August Hermann Francke</b> and Christian Thomasius.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-magdeburg">Duchy of Magdeburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.87,10.69&amp;year=1704" aria-label="Lübeck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 314 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of August Hermann Francke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.87,10.69&amp;year=1704">What was here in 1704? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
